2. Explain the key features of document-oriented databases and discuss how MongoDB implements these features.
Key features of document-oriented databases:
Flexible schema
Nested / hierarchical data (embedded documents & arrays)
High scalability and performance
Rich querying and indexing
Horizontal scaling support
How MongoDB implements them:
Flexible schema: Documents in a collection need not have the same structure. Schema validation can be optionally enforced.
Embedded documents & arrays: Allows modeling complex hierarchical data in a single document (reduces JOINs).
BSON format: Binary JSON that supports additional data types (Date, ObjectId, Binary, etc.).
Indexing: Supports single-field, compound, multikey, text, geospatial, and hashed indexes.
Aggregation Framework: Powerful pipeline for data processing, grouping, filtering, and transformation.
Sharding: Built-in horizontal scaling.
Replication: Replica sets for high availability.
3. Explain the concept of consistency in MongoDB. Discuss how MongoDB maintains data consistency in distributed environments.
Consistency means that all clients see the same data at the same time (or after a write has been acknowledged).
MongoDB offers tunable consistency:
By default, reads from the primary provide strong consistency.
Reads from secondaries can be eventually consistent.
How MongoDB maintains consistency in distributed environments:
Replica Sets: Write operations go to the primary. Secondaries asynchronously replicate the oplog. Write Concern (w: "majority") ensures the write is acknowledged by a majority of nodes before success is returned.
Read Concern: Levels such as "local", "majority", "linearizable" control how up-to-date the data must be.
Causal Consistency: Sessions can guarantee that a client sees its own writes in order.
Transactions: Multi-document ACID transactions provide strong consistency across multiple documents/collections.
4. Explain transactions in MongoDB. How does MongoDB support transactions involving multiple operations and documents?
A transaction is a sequence of operations that are executed as a single atomic unit — either all succeed or all fail (ACID properties).
MongoDB support:
Single-document operations are always atomic.
Multi-document transactions (introduced in MongoDB 4.0 for replica sets, 4.2 for sharded clusters) allow ACID guarantees across multiple documents, collections, and even databases.
How it works:
const session = db.getMongo().startSession();
session.startTransaction();
try {
  // Multiple operations
  db.orders.insertOne({ ... }, { session });
  db.inventory.updateOne({ ... }, { $inc: { qty: -1 } }, { session });
  session.commitTransaction();
} catch (error) {
  session.abortTransaction();
}
Transactions use snapshot isolation and support retryable writes.
5. Discuss the availability features of MongoDB and explain how replication helps maintain high availability.
High Availability features:
Replica Sets (primary + secondary members)
Automatic failover
Heartbeats and election process
Arbiter nodes (optional)
How replication helps:
Data is continuously replicated from primary to secondaries via the oplog.
If the primary fails, an election occurs and a secondary is promoted to primary (usually within seconds).
Clients can continue reading/writing with minimal downtime.
Read preference can be set to distribute read load to secondaries.
Minimum of 3 voting members is recommended for production for automatic failover.
6. Explain the query features of MongoDB with suitable examples of CRUD operations and filtering documents.
CRUD Operations:
Create
db.students.insertOne({ name: "Amit", age: 21, course: "CS" })
db.students.insertMany([{ name: "Riya" }, { name: "Rahul" }])
Read
db.students.find()                          // all documents
db.students.find({ age: { $gt: 20 } })      // filtering
db.students.find({ course: "CS" }, { name: 1, _id: 0 })  // projection
Update
db.students.updateOne({ name: "Amit" }, { $set: { age: 22 } })
db.students.updateMany({ course: "CS" }, { $inc: { age: 1 } })
Delete
db.students.deleteOne({ name: "Rahul" })
db.students.deleteMany({ age: { $lt: 18 } })
Filtering operators: $eq, $gt, $lt, $in, $and, $or, $regex, $exists, etc.
7. Explain MongoDB scaling mechanisms. Differentiate between vertical scaling and horizontal scaling with reference to MongoDB.
Vertical Scaling (Scale Up):
Increase resources (CPU, RAM, disk) of a single server.
MongoDB supports this, but it has hardware limits and is expensive.
Horizontal Scaling (Scale Out):
Add more servers and distribute data.
MongoDB implements this via Sharding:
Data is partitioned across multiple shards (each shard is a replica set).
A shard key determines how data is distributed.
mongos routers direct queries to the appropriate shards.
Config servers store metadata.
