14. Explain the challenges of handling complex transactions that span different operations in MongoDB. Discuss how MongoDB transactions can address these challenges.
Challenges before multi-document transactions:
No atomicity across multiple documents → partial updates possible.
Manual compensation logic required (two-phase commit pattern was complex).
Consistency issues in distributed environments.
How MongoDB transactions address them:
Full ACID multi-document transactions (replica sets & sharded clusters).
Snapshot isolation.
Automatic retry of transient errors.
Ability to abort and roll back all changes.
Support for operations across multiple collections and databases within one transaction.
Developers can now write business logic that previously required complex application-level workarounds.
15. Explain how MongoDB supports queries against varying aggregate structures. Discuss the advantages of its flexible schema with suitable examples.
MongoDB’s flexible schema allows documents in the same collection to have different structures.
Support for varying structures:
Queries work even if some fields are missing ($exists, $type).
Aggregation pipeline can handle missing or differently structured fields gracefully.
Schema validation is optional and can be partial.
Advantages with examples:
Product catalog: Electronics have screenSize, clothing has size and color — same collection.
User profiles: Some users have social links, others have address details — no null columns needed.
Event logging: Different event types carry different metadata fields.
This reduces the need for complex joins or multiple tables and speeds up development when requirements evolve.
